-**Parameters:**
-*   **Max Level ($L$):** The depth of the octree. The algorithm runs in $O(L)$ iterations [S1, unofficial][S2].
-*   **Resolution:** Demonstrated at $128K^3$ ($131,072^3$) voxel grids [S2].
-*   **Sub-DAG Threshold:** For resolutions over $1K^3$, generate sub-DAGs of that size first to manage memory [S2].
-
-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Memory Constraints:** The complete SVO may be orders of magnitude larger than the final DAG. The method must support streaming or chunk-based processing (constructing sub-DAGs) to handle scenes that do not fit in RAM or on disk [S1, unofficial][S2].
-*   **Attribute Compression:** The method focuses on geometry (visibility). It does not compress material or reflectance properties (color, normal) [S2]. Decoupling attributes is handled by [Voxel Attribute Decoupling](1761-voxel-attribute-decoupling.md) [G].
-*   **Precision:** Pointer indirection must be exact to preserve geometry.
+### Parameters and Edge Cases
+-   **Max Level ($L$):** The maximum depth of the octree. The algorithm is guaranteed to find the minimal DAG within $L$ iterations [S1, unofficial][S2].
+-   **Resolution:** Demonstrated at $128K^3$ (131,072$^3$) voxels [S2].
+-   **Edge Case - Irregular Data:** The method works on irregular data because it decouples voxel content (geometry) from spatial position, unlike earlier methods that only worked on axis-aligned regular datasets [S1, unofficial].
+-   **Edge Case - Memory:** If the SVO does not fit in memory, the chunking strategy (Step 1) must be used. The algorithm handles partially reduced DAGs as input [S1, unofficial][S2].
+-   **Pitfall - Attributes:** The method focuses on geometry. It does not compress material or reflectance properties (color, normals). If attributes are present, they must be handled separately (e.g., via Voxel Attribute Decoupling) [S2].
 
